  "progress": "Pitch: matches a note some of the time (self-rated). Starts with the smooth spot over a drone in C and D. Next: hold a step in tune in F and G.\nIntervals & leaps: sings up and down by step, Do Re Mi. Starts with Do to Re and back, then a step down by ear. Next: a 3rd up, and steps over chords.\nBreath & range: runs out of breath on long notes. Starts with a 2 to 5 count breath, then a long Do. Next: a 3 to 6 count breath, and steps held on one breath.\nMelodies: not answered, so untested. Starts with easy Do Re and Mi tunes in C, F and G. Watch whether a tune comes without the lead.\nRuns: not answered, untested. One easy five-note run on day 5. Stop a pass that feels high.\nHarmony & improvising: not answered, untested. Steps over C, F and G chords on days 2, 3, 6 and 7.\nEar: step up by ear (M2), then 3rds and 5ths up (M3, P5). Next: steps down by ear.\nNotes: alto. Keep notes low in the range, and sing on oo when a note sits high.",
  "days": [
    {
      "theme": "Home, and one step up",
      "exercises": [
        {
          "title": "Find the smooth spot",
          "minutes": 3,
          "level": "beginner",
          "area": "voice",
          "tags": ["sing", "ear"],
          "topic": "voice-pitch",
          "concepts": ["matching-a-pitch"],
          "why": "A note in tune with the drone sounds smooth, and an off note wobbles against it, so the drone shows you when you are on the note.",
          "learnBefore": ["/course/3/matching-a-pitch"],
          "cells": [
            {"type": "text", "text": "Press Start exercise below: the drone and the mic start together, and your pitch shows as a line on the staff, the five lines of music. Sing the low {{1}} on \"oo\" and slide a little above and below it until the wobble stops. Hold the smooth spot for as long as feels easy."},
            {"type": "tool", "tool": {"type": "staff", "scale": "major-ionian", "key": "c", "voice": "alto", "enable": ["1"], "others": "hidden", "labels": "solfege", "drone": true}}
          ]
        },
        {
          "title": "Breathe, then hold Do",
          "minutes": 4,
          "level": "new",
          "area": "voice",
          "tags": ["sing"],
          "topic": "voice-breath-range",
          "concepts": ["breath-holding-note"],
          "why": "A note stays steady while the breath under it stays calm, so settle the breath before you hold the note.",
          "learnBefore": ["/course/3/breath-and-holding-a-note"],
          "cells": [
            {
              "type": "section",
              "heading": "Breathe in and out",
              "minutes": 1,
              "cells": [
                {"type": "text", "text": "Press Start in the circle below. Breathe in as the circle grows, hold, then breathe out as it shrinks, each for the count shown. Keep your shoulders still and notice where the breath goes."},
                {"type": "tool", "tool": {"type": "breathwork", "level": "new"}}
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "section",
              "heading": "Hold the low Do",
              "minutes": 3,
              "cells": [
                {"type": "text", "text": "Press Start exercise below: the drone and the mic start together. Sing the low {{1}} on \"oo\" and hold it while your line stays flat. Breathe when you need to, then go again."},
                {"type": "tool", "tool": {"type": "staff", "scale": "major-ionian", "key": "c", "voice": "alto", "enable": ["1"], "others": "hidden", "labels": "solfege", "drone": true}}
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "title": "One step up and back",
          "minutes": 5,
          "level": "new",
          "area": "voice",
          "tags": ["sing"],
          "topic": "voice-intervals",
          "concepts": ["do-re-mi-steps"],
          "why": "Re is the next note up from Do, so going up one step and back is the first move most tunes make.",
          "learnBefore": ["/course/3/do-re-mi"],
          "cells": [
